Q: How do I print/display an HTML Element, using an embedded JavaScript code inside of HTML? A: <p>Firstly, you don't want to use <code>innerHTML</code> if you're just putting text in there.  The reason is that if someone has reserved characters, or even script in their username, they can hijack your page.  Use <code>textContent</code> instead.</p>
<p>Next, you have the right idea, but it's probably easiest/best to show/hide after the timing.  You can do this with CSS animations, or with some simple script:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const usernameEl = document.querySelector('.username');
usernameEl.style.visibility = false;
usernameEl.textContent = 'some username';

setTimeout(() =&gt; usernameEl.style.visibility = true, 5000);
setTimeout(() =&gt; usernameEl.style.visibility = false, 10_000);
</code></pre>
<p>Note that if you're timing this with an animation video, you might consider using the <code>currentTime</code> of that video to set the trigger, in case, the video buffers or plays at a different rate than expected.  You might also consider setting the <code>z-index</code> so that your animation overlay displays on top no matter what, and then you don't need to worry about the visibility of the username.</p>

Q: Multi-responsibility model method in CodeIgniter is not performing in accordance with parameter type and quality rules A: <p>Your problem with this script is the number 1 reason why having a multi-responsibility model method is a bad idea. Your Swiss Army knife method can do so many things that it has become difficult to manage.</p>
<p>The call site of this script doesn't consistently cast numeric strings as integers, so your model method is performing unexpectedly.</p>
<p>I strongly recommend a refactor.  Create separate methods which have a clear behavior and can be easily maintained and unit tested.</p>
<p>These methods should atomic (do only one thing), have a single parameter with a single datatype, and have a narrow return type.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getByUserId(int $id): ?object
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']}&quot; =&gt; $id]
        )
        -&gt;row();
}

public function getByUserName(string $userName): ?object
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['username']}&quot; =&gt; $userName]
        )
        -&gt;row();
}

public function getUserGroupId(string $groupName): ?int
{
     return $this-&gt;constantsServices[$groupName] ?? null;
}

public function getUsersByGroupId(int $groupId): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id_user_groups']}&quot; =&gt; $groupId]
        )
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<p>With these methods in place, your controller method(s) can implement your rules with absolute clarity.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function endPoint($value)
{
    if (ctype_digit($value) {
        $user = $this-&gt;User_model-&gt;getByUserId((int)$value);
    } elseif ($groupId = $this-&gt;User_model-&gt;getUserGroupId($value)) {
        $users = $this-&gt;User_model-&gt;getUsersByGroupId($groupId);
    } else {
        $user = $this-&gt;User_model-&gt;getByUserName($value);
    }
    // Do something with $user/$users
}
</code></pre>
<p>Additional tips:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>select('*')</code> can be safely omitted; it is the default.</li>
<li><code>get_where()</code> can be used to consolidate <code>where()</code>, <code>from()</code>, and <code>get()</code>.</li>
</ul>

Q: Implement an arbitrary order to my enum fields? A: <p>As others have already stated, the standard way of doing this is to implement <code>Ord</code> and <code>PartialOrd</code> on the struct. This will ensure ordering in the same way you defined your enum. The code generated by these macros can be seen below.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>#[derive(Eq, PartialEq, Ord, PartialOrd)]
enum Fruit {
    Apple,
    Banana,
    Kiwi,
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>#[automatically_derived]
impl ::core::cmp::Ord for Fruit {
    #[inline]
    fn cmp(&amp;self, other: &amp;Fruit) -&gt; ::core::cmp::Ordering {
        let __self_discr = ::core::intrinsics::discriminant_value(self);
        let __arg1_discr = ::core::intrinsics::discriminant_value(other);
        ::core::cmp::Ord::cmp(&amp;__self_discr, &amp;__arg1_discr)
    }
}

#[automatically_derived]
impl ::core::cmp::PartialOrd for Fruit {
    #[inline]
    fn partial_cmp(
        &amp;self,
        other: &amp;Fruit,
    ) -&gt; ::core::option::Option&lt;::core::cmp::Ordering&gt; {
        let __self_discr = ::core::intrinsics::discriminant_value(self);
        let __arg1_discr = ::core::intrinsics::discriminant_value(other);
        ::core::cmp::PartialOrd::partial_cmp(&amp;__self_discr, &amp;__arg1_discr)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>What you are looking at here is the code generated by the derive macros. Rust enum variants get an integer value assigned at compile time by the rules defined here: <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/items/enumerations.html#custom-discriminant-values-for-fieldless-enumerations" rel="nofollow noreferrer">rust-lang.org</a>. These discriminants are guaranteed to be unique, and if you don't assign them manually as shown below, they will always increase by one. It doesn't matter if it is a variant with fields or just a regular one like in this example. <code>discriminant_value </code>retrieves that integer, and from this point on the values can just be sorted by it. Normally, just using the macros is sufficient, but if you want more control over the exact order, it is also possible to write:</p>
<pre><code>#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Ord, PartialOrd, Debug)]
enum Fruit {
    Apple = 1,
    Banana = 2,
    Kiwi = 0,
}

fn main() {
    let mut fruits = [Fruit::Apple, Fruit::Banana, Fruit::Kiwi];
    fruits.sort();
    assert_eq!(
        fruits,
        [Fruit::Kiwi, Fruit::Apple, Fruit::Banana]
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>The following approach isn't something I would recommend unless you have a very good reason to do so (for example, two values that should be equal). It is possible to implement the Ord trait manually and define an outcome for every possible combination. An example of a complex ordering is shown below:</p>
<pre><code>use std::cmp::Ordering;

#[derive(PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
enum Fruit {
    Apple,
    Banana,
    Kiwi,
}

impl Fruit {
    fn rank(&amp;self) -&gt; usize {
        match self {
            Fruit::Apple =&gt; 1,
            Fruit::Banana =&gt; 1,
            Fruit::Kiwi =&gt; 0,
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut values = vec![Fruit::Apple, Fruit::Banana, Fruit::Kiwi];
    values.sort_by(|a, b| a.rank().cmp(&amp;b.rank()));
    assert_eq!(
        values,
        vec![Fruit::Kiwi, Fruit::Apple, Fruit::Banana]
    );
}
</code></pre>
